> On Dec 30, 2023, at 2:12=E2=80=AFPM, Jason Thorpe <thorpej@me.com> =
wrote:
>=20
> Ok, I think I=E2=80=99ve narrowed it down to what appears to be some =
sort of overflow problem when setting up the kernel L2 PTEs.  What =
puzzles me is that this code hasn=E2=80=99t changed in a long time, and =
obviously worked for you before=E2=80=A6 but the compiler is now =
different perhaps?
>=20
> Anyway=E2=80=A6 I should have it fixed shortly, and hopefully the gods =
will look upon me favorably and I can sneak the fix into -10.

Ok, found it.  Somehow before we were just getting lucky I guess that =
large memory configs weren=E2=80=99t pushing us past a single L2 kernel =
page table.  The kernel *thought* it was all set for a large kernel VA =
space, but the page tables initialized incorrectly. =20

The macro used to index the kernel L2 PTs to poke the value in them to =
point to the L3 PTs was overflowing from 1023->0 because it was designed =
to, and it probably just shouldn=E2=80=99t have been used in this one =
particular spot.  The fix is quite simple, and the comment explaining it =
is more complicated than the change :-)


<quote>
@@ -1358,8 +1360,19 @@ pmap_bootstrap(paddr_t ptaddr, u_int max
  pte =3D (ALPHA_K0SEG_TO_PHYS(((vaddr_t)lev3map) +
      (i*PAGE_SIZE)) >> PGSHIFT) << PG_SHIFT;
  pte |=3D PG_V | PG_ASM | PG_KRE | PG_KWE | PG_WIRED;
- lev2map[l2pte_index(VM_MIN_KERNEL_ADDRESS+
-     (i*PAGE_SIZE*NPTEPG))] =3D pte;
+ /*
+ * No need to use l2pte_index() here; it's equivalent
+ * to just indexing with our loop variable i, but will
+ * fall over if we end up with more than 1 L2 PT page.
+ *
+ * In other words:
+ *
+ * l2pte_index(VM_MIN_KERNEL_ADDRESS +
+ *             (i*PAGE_SIZE*NPTEPG))
+ *
+ * ...is the same as 'i' so long as i stays below 1024.
+ */
+ lev2map[i] =3D pte;
  }
    /* Initialize the pmap_growkernel_lock. */
</quote>

>=20
> -- thorpej
>=20

-- thorpej



